Yes, for the coding part of Phase 1, it is literally just creating those 5 Java files.
It might feel "too simple" right now, but that is the secret to building massive enterprise features: you have to build the "chess pieces" before you try to write the rules of the game. If you try to write the complex recursive algorithms and the data models at the same time, your brain will get overwhelmed.
By defining these 5 files first, you are creating a clean, safe sandbox for your algorithm to play in.
Here is the complete, clean Java code for your Phase 1 models. You can create a new package (e.g., com.devcollab.service.planner.model) and drop these in.
### 1. PlanningTask.java
This is the stripped-down version of your database Task. Notice how it only contains the exact fields needed to do math and check constraints.
```java
package com.devcollab.service.planner.model;

import lombok.Builder;
import lombok.Data;
import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

@Data
@Builder
public class PlanningTask {
    private Long taskId;
    private String title;
    private double estimatedHours;
    private String priority; // CRITICAL, HIGH, MEDIUM, LOW
    private LocalDate deadline;
    
    // Algorithm needs these to check constraints!
    private Set<String> requiredSkills; 
    private List<Long> dependencies; // List of Task IDs this task depends on
}

```
### 2. TeamMember.java
This strips away the passwords, emails, and profile pictures. The algorithm only cares about their capacity and what they know how to do.
```java
package com.devcollab.service.planner.model;

import lombok.Builder;
import lombok.Data;
import java.util.Set;

@Data
@Builder
public class TeamMember {
    private Long memberId;
    private double availableHours; // e.g., 40.0 for a standard week
    private Set<String> skills;    // e.g., ["Java", "React", "SQL"]
}

```
### 3. Assignment.java
This is the core building block of the solution. It links one Task to one Developer.
```java
package com.devcollab.service.planner.model;

import lombok.Builder;
import lombok.Data;
import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.util.Set;

@Data
@Builder
public class Assignment {
    private Long taskId;
    private Long developerId; // Null means "Leave in Backlog" (UNASSIGNED)
    
    // We copy these here so the Constraint Engine can read them instantly
    private double estimatedHours;
    private Set<String> requiredSkills;
    private LocalDate deadline;
    private String priority;
}

```
### 4. Constraint.java (Interface)
This is the Strategy Pattern contract. We will build 5 different classes that implement this interface in Phase 3.
```java
package com.devcollab.service.planner.model;

public interface Constraint {
    /**
     * Checks if a proposed assignment breaks this specific rule.
     * @return true if valid, false if it breaks the rule
     */
    boolean isSatisfied(Assignment assignment);
    
    String getName(); // e.g., "CapacityConstraint" or "SkillConstraint"
}

```
### 5. SprintPlanResult.java
This is the final DTO that gets sent back to the frontend so the Project Manager can review the algorithm's hard work.
```java
package com.devcollab.service.planner.model;

import lombok.Builder;
import lombok.Data;
import java.util.List;

@Data
@Builder
public class SprintPlanResult {
    private Long sprintId;
    private int planScore; // 0 to 100
    private int tasksAssigned;
    private int tasksOverflow; // How many couldn't fit
    
    private List<Assignment> assignments;
    private List<String> warnings; // e.g., "Dev A is at 98% capacity"
}

```
Once you have these files created in your IDE, Phase 1 is officially 100% complete.
